---
import Snippet from '../../components/docs/Snippet.astro';
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';

const refdes = 'text-accent m-0 mb-4 font-mono text-sm font-normal tracking-normal';

const launcherScaffold = `bun create bridgething my-hub --launcher`;

const launcherManifest = `{
  "id": "…",
  "name": "My Hub",
  "version": "0.1.0",
  "role": "launcher"
}`;

const launcherSdk = `// the daemon hides launcher-role bundles from this list
const list = await client.webapp.list();
const current = await client.webapp.current();

const icon = await client.webapp.icon({ id: info.id });
await client.webapp.activate({ id: info.id });

// handle new installations and uninstalls
client.webapp.onWebappInstalled(reload);
client.webapp.onWebappUninstalled(reload);`;

const overlayScaffold = `bun create bridgething my-overlay --overlay`;

const overlayManifest = `{
  "id": "…",
  "name": "My Overlay",
  "version": "0.1.0",
  "overlay": "overlay.js"
}`;

const overlayGlobal = `// the daemon prepends this before your bundle
window.__bridgethingOverlay = {
  origin: 'http://127.0.0.1:8891',
  surfaces: {
    notifications: true,
    call: true,
    pairing: true,
    connection: true,
    volume: false, // this app draws its own volume indicator
  },
};`;

const overlayBoot = `const cfg = window.__bridgethingOverlay;
if (!cfg || !location.origin.startsWith(cfg.origin)) return; // not a page the daemon served
if (window.__bridgethingOverlayMounted) return;               // double injection is a no-op
window.__bridgethingOverlayMounted = true;

if (cfg.surfaces.volume) wireVolume(client);
if (cfg.surfaces.pairing) wirePairing(client);`;
---

<DocsLayout
  title="launchers and overlays"
  description="replace the built-in hub with your own home screen, and the built-in system overlay with your own."
  current="launchers-and-overlays">
  <header class="mb-12">
    <h1>launchers and overlays</h1>
    <p class="text-white/60">
      the hub and the system overlay are just webapps. you can build your own and mark them as launchers and overlays.
    </p>
  </header>

  <section class="mb-14">
    <h2 class={refdes}>s1 - two slots</h2>
    <p class="text-white/60">a slot names one installed bundle for a system role. there are two:</p>
    <ul role="list" class="mt-4 space-y-1.5 text-sm text-white/60">
      <li>
        <strong class="text-white/80">launcher</strong> - the home screen, accessed by pressing the menu button 5 times.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="text-white/80">overlay</strong> - the ui drawn on top of <em>every</em> webapp: notification toasts,
        the call banner, the pairing pin, the disconnected banner, the volume indicator.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="mt-4 text-white/60">
      you set both from the companion phone app. an unset slot means the built-in provider.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-14">
    <h2 class={refdes}>s2 - build a launcher</h2>
    <Snippet code={launcherScaffold} lang="bash" />
    <p class="mt-3 text-white/60">
      the scaffold declares <code>"role": "launcher"</code>. that does two things: the daemon hides the bundle from
      <code>client.webapp.list</code>, and it makes the bundle eligible for the launcher slot.
    </p>
    <Snippet code={launcherManifest} lang="json" />
    <p class="mt-3 text-white/60">
      everything a home screen needs is on the normal client sdk. the grid is four calls and two events:
    </p>
    <Snippet code={launcherSdk} />
    <p class="mt-3 text-white/60">
      the built-in hub also does bluetooth bonds and pairing name, display brightness, system health, power, and ota
      progress - we recommend yours does as well.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-14">
    <h2 class={refdes}>s3 - build an overlay</h2>
    <Snippet code={overlayScaffold} lang="bash" />
    <Snippet code={overlayManifest} lang="json" />
    <p class="mt-3 text-white/60">
      <code>overlay</code> points at one self-contained script. when your bundle holds the overlay slot, the daemon injects
      that file into every webapp's document as it loads. any bundle can carry an overlay, but it's usually something a hub
      would do.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-white/60">the daemon prepends one global before your code:</p>
    <Snippet code={overlayGlobal} />
    <p class="mt-3 text-white/60">
      <strong class="text-white/80">honor <code>surfaces</code>.</strong> an app that draws its own volume indicator sets
      <code>volume: false</code>. the daemon cannot enforce this, but you will get duplicate ui if you ignore it.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-white/60">the starter encodes four things worth keeping:</p>
    <Snippet code={overlayBoot} />
    <ul role="list" class="mt-4 space-y-1.5 text-sm text-white/60">
      <li><strong class="text-white/80">origin guard</strong> - never run in a page the daemon did not serve.</li>
      <li><strong class="text-white/80">mount guard</strong> - a second injection must be a no-op.</li>
      <li>
        <strong class="text-white/80">closed shadow root</strong> - so your styles and the host app's cannot reach each other.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="text-white/80">escape-only, capture-phase keys</strong>, and only while something is showing.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-white/60">
      the hard constraint: <code>overlay.js</code> must be <strong class="text-white/80">one self-contained file</strong
      >
      under 512&nbsp;KiB.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 class={refdes}>s4 - assign it, and get back</h2>
    <p class="text-white/60">
      install your bundle the usual way, then open the companion app and pick it under home screen and overlay.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-white/60">there are three ways back to the built-in:</p>
    <ul role="list" class="mt-4 space-y-1.5 text-sm text-white/60">
      <li><strong class="text-white/80">clear the slot</strong> in the companion app.</li>
      <li><strong class="text-white/80">uninstall the bundle</strong></li>
      <li><strong class="text-white/80">factory reset</strong></li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</DocsLayout>
